    "result_text": "There's only one test file, and it passes along with a clean build. \n\n## Summary\n\nThe change adds a derived, read-only `membershipNumber` field formatted `<customerCode>-M<YY>` (YY = last two digits of the registration date's year, e.g. `SMI-0007-M26`).\n\nI followed the existing pattern for the other computed owner fields (`displayName`, `initials`, `householdId`, `customerCode`), touching exactly **two files**:\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the `membershipNumber` read-only property to the `Owner` schema, which generates the DTO field.\n2. **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 added a single `@Mapping` expression alongside the existing computed-field mappings:\n   ```java\n   @Mapping(target = \"membershipNumber\", expression = \"java(owner.getCustomerCode() + \\\"-M\\\" + String.format(\\\"%02d\\\", owner.getRegistrationDate().getYear() % 100))\")\n   ```\n\n**On structural impact:** I initially delegated to a new `MembershipNumber` util class, but reverted to an inline expression. The util would have added a third touched file (the cost multiplier) without lowering the dominant term \u2014 the change to `toOwnerDto` is a single annotation line against `OwnerMapper`'s existing complexity either way. Inlining keeps it at 2 files, one trivial one-line change, and mirrors how `displayName`/`initials` are already computed. No method or class grew in complexity.\n\nBuild passes, `AcceptanceTest.coreMembershipNumberFormat` green.",
